    { q: "What do we learn about Mrs Lindqvist in the fourth paragraph?",
      options: ["She regretted moving to a smaller house.", "She disagreed with her children about her playing.", "She missed teaching the piano.", "She wanted to start teaching again."],
      answer: 1, explain: "'They think I should stop playing… But if I stop, my hands will stop too.'" },
    { q: "How had the narrator's attitude to the piano changed by the end of the fifth paragraph?",
      options: ["He had started to look forward to it.", "He still found it impossible to work.", "He had learnt to accept it.", "He had begun to enjoy it very much."],
      answer: 2, explain: "The scales 'bothered me less' and 'had become part of the morning', but 'I would not say I had grown to enjoy them', so A and D go too far." },
    { q: "Why did the narrator knock on Mrs Lindqvist's door at the end?",
      options: ["to complain about the noise", "to find out what a piece of music was", "to ask her to teach him the piano", "to tell her how much he admired her"],
      answer: 1, explain: "'I knocked on her door to ask her what the piece had been.' It was she who mentioned learning to play (C)." }
